  "worked_solution": "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆{A}^{2}\n$$\n\nWe also have the following notations:\n\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\in R$\n\n- $x\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\notin R$\n\nIn this case, we have $X\\,R\\,Y$ if and only if $Y⊇X\\cup Y.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. In this case, we have ${Q}^{-}⊇{Q}^{+}\\cup {Q}^{-}.$ This is equivalent to ${Q}^{+}\\cup {Q}^{-}⊆{Q}^{-},$ which is false.\n\n- Statement II is true. In this case, we have $Q⊇{Q}^{-}\\cup Q.$ This is equivalent to ${Q}^{-}\\cup Q⊆Q,$ which is true.\n\n- Statement III is true. In this case, we have ${Q}^{+}⊉Q\\cup {Q}^{+}.$ This is equivalent to $Q\\cup {Q}^{+}⊈{Q}^{+},$ which is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"",
